speechtext:
Yes. As regards the application of the Act, it applies solely to people engaged in commercial or manufacturing pursuits, not, as the English Act, I believe, to anybody who wishes to take advantage of it. Section 3 says :
This Act applies only to persons who as a means of livelihood, manufacture, buy, or otherwise acquire goods, wares, merchandise or commodities, ordinarily the subject of trade and commerce, and sell or otherwise dispose of the same to others, including commission merchants, whether they sell by auction or otherwise.
By another section of the Act it is declared not to apply to incorporated companies which may fall under the provisions of the Winding-up Act. The second part of the Bill defines what constitutes insolvency. It provides that a person to whom the Act applies commits an Act of insolvency in any of the following cases :
(a.) If he ceases to meet his liabilities generally as they come due ;
(b.) If he calls a meeting of his creditors for the purposes of compounding with them ; or if he exhibits a statement showing his inability to meet his liabilities ; or if he gives notice to any of his creditors that he has suspended, cr is about to suspend payment of his debts ; or if he otherwise acknowledges his Insolvency ;
